t and since we  run doodles on google.com any downtime  would be really bad it could put the  project in jeopardy because we can't  exactly just move Halloween back a  couple days but luckily we only had to  keep this thing alive for 48 hours so  unlike most game developers who have to  support a project for months or years we  only had to stay awake for two days so  I'll just briefly run through our  back-end infrastructure you may remember  a similar diagram from earlier this is  what we started with then we met Joe he  built us a matchmaker and this is  essentially what the inside of our  clusters look like at launch when we  needed to scale up capacity we picked  regions around the globe to start  clusters in we connected them to clouds  global load balancer and then we just  let it balance the traffic  geographically we didn't have any custom  logic for routing people to different  regions and when we needed to scale it  further we just added more clusters  so here we have a list of all of the  tools and technology that we use to  develop the backends as you can see here  everything that we used is available  externally  there's no Google specific internal  magic behind the scenes and even the way  we interacted with cloud was very  similar to how an external customer  would have interacted with them so go a  little bit into our development process  for those that are interested we  separated our development into two GCP  projects one for production one for  development and we kept all of the  resources separate between these  projects which ensured that anything we  were doing in development wouldn't  suddenly break production and within our  development development project we had  our own clusters for development so I  had a cluster Jonathan had a cluster we  had a cluster for load testing and it  was really important that we had no  special configuration for these they  were basically production clusters but  we wanted to make sure that behavior in  these clusters were as as close to prod  as possible and finally we wanted to  make the process of creating and  destroying them really easy because when  you end up in a weird state after hours  of debugging the last thing you want to  do is to have to figure out you know how  do I fix this in real time it's nice to  be able to just throw everything away  and start from scratch  so we wrote a couple of scripts to make  this process easy okay so we have a game  we have a plan for how to scale things  how do we actually figure out how many  servers we need so in our case we knew  roughly how much traffic to expect by  looking at numbers from previous doodles  we knew how long our games were and if  we combine these two we could use some  basic math to estimate how many people  would be connected at a given time so we  did that we extrapolated a little bit  and we came up with an initial estimate  we knew our numbers wouldn't be perfect  but we just hope that they would be  close enough just to get a rough idea of  how much traffic to expect okay so now  we have our initial estimate we need to  figure out if we can actually handle  that much traffic so we wrote a custom  client a headless client to simulate the  process of  connecting to the matchmaker joining  into a game playing through it and then  disconnecting and then when we wanted to  run load tests we just started a bunch  of these in a kubernetes job pointed it  at our load test cluster and then let  him run wild and our strategy throughout  this whole process was to start really  small only scale up when we are  confident that the system could handle a  given amount of traffic so here we have  a quick example we have ten clients per  second pointed a cluster here when we're  confident that it can handle that much  traffic we scale that up to 20 looks  good at 20 so we go to 30 and then in  this example we noticed that at 30  clients per second our matchmaker starts  having issues so let's say our CPU usage  is spiking  we investigate we decide that we just  need more matchmakers so we had more we  rerun our tests we make sure that it's  stable and if everything looks good we  carry on and then occasionally we would  run longer tests because there are  certain things that you can only figure  out after maybe 10 or 12 hours of load  that you wouldn't be able to see in an  hour test so we would run tests for 12  hours or 24 hours to verify that these  issues weren't happening and finally  since our estimates initially were  pretty rough we wanted to make sure that  we ran load tests well above our peak  traffic numbers our expected peak  traffic numbers and so in our case we we  scaled up our load tests to double what  we expected at peak traffic and that  basically gave us an idea of what would  happen if the doodle went viral okay so  we're ready for production at this point  we set up monitoring and alerting so  that we can have some visibility into  how the launch is going we switched from  running Redis internally in our clusters  to using cloud memory store and that  provided us better guarantees of  reliability  it gave us people we could talk to if  issues came up and then we also worked  with cloud professional services to come  up with a launch plan they helped us  with stuff like capacity planning  reserving resources setting up on-call  rotations that kind of stuff okay  launch time time to turn everything on  and hope for the best  so we wanted to spin up our clusters far  enough ahead of time to ensure that they  were stable before the doodle actually  went live on the other hand we didn't  want to give too much lead time because  running 36,000 CPUs doing nothing is  kind of expensive since we were only  running for two days we gave ourselves  about three hours to wrap everything up  I would like to have maybe four or five  but it was almost enough from there we  just kept a close eye close eye on  stackdriver and if any metrics came up  that we hadn't planned on stackdriver  allowed us to create dashboards for  those in real-time which was really nice  we worked with cloud professional  services to fix any issues as they arose  and as I mentioned earlier we scaled our  load tests up to double our expected  peak traffic while our average load  ended up being about double what we  expected so it was really valuable to  have seen how the system dealt with this  amount of traffic beforehand as opposed  to having to deal with that for the  first time on launch day we had to add a  couple more clusters to compensate  luckily though the process for spinning  those up only took about 15 minutes and  so it ended up not being that big of a  deal and we were able to do that without  any downtime and other than that the  launch went pretty smoothly so just a  quick summary to wrap things up our  back-end and front-end engineers worked  very closely together and that fact was  very valuable because the close  communication helped us avoid a lot of  design churn and it kept our kept our  final designs simple I know this is a  cloud sponsored talk but as a team that  doesn't normally think about cloud at  all or work with cloud at all we found  it a really solid base for this type of  project we never had to manage any VMs  migrate Services deal with downtime and  the cloud documentation made kubernetes  surprisingly easy to learn  speaking of kubernetes kubernetes Cooper  Nettie's made our scaling process a lot  simpler which was really useful on  launch day once we had our clusters  configured it was easily it was easy to  just start more of them to handle the  unexpected load and we can do that  without downtime  and lastly just a shout-out to Nita mang  who is our contact from cloud  professional services she did a lot of  work to help make sure this launch went  smoothly and she was a huge part of its  success and if you're interested in  learning more Google cloud wrote up a  blog post about the project which you  can find if you google Halloween doodle  GCP and if you want to play the game you  can go to google.com slash doodles and  it'll be in our archive page thank you  [Applause]
 

